{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":2,"output":1242,"cacheRead":10216,"cacheWrite":428,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":11888,"cost":{"input":0.00002,"output":0.0621,"cacheRead":0.010216,"cacheWrite":0.00535,"total":0.07768599999999999}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":0,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01Dfn6KhxD2w1kynG3DdsHUN","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"benchmark/fixtures/T10-event-projector/INCIDENT.md","content":"# Event projector incident\n\nStatus: resolved\n\n## Impact\n\nTwo production-shaped failures in the synthetic trade event projector:\n\n1. After a single temporary persistence rejection, the projector stopped\n   accepting all subsequent events for every trade (permanent poisoning), and\n   the failed event's state had already been committed in memory, diverging\n   from the store.\n2. All trades shared one global promise chain, so a slow or blocked\n   persistence call for one trade halted progress for every unrelated trade.\n\nValidation gaps also allowed version gaps, out-of-order first events,\nevents after cancellation, invalid payloads, and unknown event types to be\nsilently applied, and internal state was mutated before persistence.\n\n## Root cause\n\n- One module-wide `pending` promise chained every `project` call for all\n  trades. When any link rejected, the rejected promise remained the chain\n  tail, so every later `.then` also rejected — the queue was poisoned and it\n  was global, not per trade.\n- `states.set(...)` ran before `await applyToStore(...)`, committing state\n  that the store rejected.\n- `reduceEvent` performed no ordering, terminality, or payload validation,\n  and events were consumed by reference (mutable input exposure).\n\n## Fix\n\nIn `event-projector.mjs`:\n\n- Replaced the global chain with a per-trade queue map. Each trade chains its\n  own events (serial per trade, concurrent across trades) and the queue tail\n  is settled via a swallow handler so a rejection never blocks later events.\n- Moved `states.set` after a successful `await applyToStore`, so state\n  commits only on persistence success.\n- Added full validation: structural checks (`TypeError` for malformed\n  events/unsupported types), first event must be `CREATED` at version 1 with\n  positive finite `notional` and non-empty `owner`, subsequent events must be\n  exactly `current version + 1`, duplicate/stale versions return the current\n  snapshot without persisting, `CANCELLED` is terminal, and `AMENDED`\n  payload fields are validated to keep state complete and valid.\n- Defensive copies everywhere: events are `structuredClone`d on intake, and\n  the callback state, returned states, and snapshots are all clones, so no\n  mutable internal state escapes.\n\n## Regression coverage\n\n`student-tests.mjs` (six tests, all node:test):\n\n1. Different trades progress concurrently instead of sharing one queue\n   (a blocked persistence call for one trade does not stall another).\n2. Duplicate/stale versions return the current snapshot without persisting.\n3. Version gaps and invalid first events reject without state change, and a\n   rejection does not poison later valid work for the same trade.\n4. A cancelled trade is terminal and rejects a later new version.\n5. Malformed events and invalid payload values reject with `TypeError`\n   without changing state.\n6. Mutating input events, callback state, returned state, or snapshots does\n   not affect internal state.\n\n## Remaining risk\n\n- Persistence and in-memory commit are not atomic: if the process dies after\n  `applyToStore` succeeds but before `states.set`, memory lags the store\n  until restart/replay. Acceptable for this in-memory fixture.\n- If `applyToStore` succeeds but the store later loses the write, the\n  projector has no reconciliation; recovery would need replay from the store.\n- Version numbers are validated as positive integers; extremely large trade\n  cardinality would grow the `states`/`queues` maps unbounded (no eviction).\n"}}}}
